<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Theodor Herzl</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1860">1860</a>–<a href="/years/1904">1904</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Herzl</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Theodor Herzl was a Jewish journalist and writer born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a> and active in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, regarded as the founder of modern political <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zionism">Zionism</a>. In works such as The Jewish State (1896) he called for a Jewish national homeland, and he convened the first Zionist Congress in 1897. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a> name him among the figures produced by the cosmopolitan culture of Vienna.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/theodor-herzl.jpg" alt="Theodor Herzl" width="316" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carl Pietzner · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Theodor_Herzl_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Vienna's fin-de-siècle culture rested on this prosperity: the Ringstrasse, the Secession movement, coffee-house intellectual life and the professional class that produced Sigmund Freud, Gustav Mahler, Gustav Klimt and <b>Theodor Herzl</b> all belonged to a prosperous, cosmopolitan urban economy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Theodor Herzl</b>, unaware of this, popularized the word himself, and did not call himself a Zionist until months after publishing Der Judenstaat in 1896.</blockquote><a href="/movement/zionism">Zionism · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pest-hungary">Pest</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/hungary">Hungary</a>. Died in Edlach an der Rax, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.5,19.1&amp;year=1888" aria-label="Pest on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4996 498 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Theodor Herzl"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5446 768h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5446 768h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.5,19.1&amp;year=1888">What was here in 1888? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;We are one people—our enemies have made us one without our consent, as repeatedly happens in history. Distress binds us together, and, thus united, we suddenly discover our strength.&#8221;</p><footer>The Jewish State (1896), ch. 2 &#183; tr. Sylvie d'Avigdor, 1896 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Jewish_State_(1896_translation)/The_Jewish_Question" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;We shall live at last as free men on our own soil, and die peacefully in our own homes.&#8221;</p><footer>The Jewish State (1896), conclusion &#183; tr. Sylvie d'Avigdor, 1896 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Jewish_State_(1896_translation)/Conclusion" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The world will be freed by our liberty, enriched by our wealth, magnified by our greatness.&#8221;</p><footer>The Jewish State (1896), conclusion &#183; tr. Sylvie d'Avigdor, 1896 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Jewish_State_(1896_translation)/Conclusion" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>2 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Therefore I believe that a wondrous generation of Jews will spring into existence. The Maccabeans will rise again.&#8221;</p><footer>The Jewish State (1896), conclusion &#183; tr. Sylvie d'Avigdor, 1896 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Jewish_State_(1896_translation)/Conclusion" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;No one can deny the gravity of the situation of the Jews. Wherever they live in perceptible numbers, they are more or less persecuted.&#8221;</p><footer>The Jewish State (1896), ch. 2 &#183; tr. Sylvie d'Avigdor, 1896 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Jewish_State_(1896_translation)/The_Jewish_Question" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
